          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How wide should a walkway be?",
            "answer": "<p>Three feet is the practical minimum for one person; four feet lets two people walk side by side and is the usual choice for a front entry. We'll mark it out on site so you can see it before we form.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you pour a walkway with a wheelbarrow-only access?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Narrow side-yard runs are a big share of what we do — we wheelbarrow, pump or chute depending on the site and tell you the plan at the estimate.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why do walkways develop lips and trip hazards?",
            "answer": "<p>Usually a weak or uneven base letting one panel settle. We compact the full run and space control joints properly so the slabs move together instead of against each other.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can my walkway match my patio?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes — whether that's a matching broom finish or the same stamp pattern and color. See our <a href=\"/pages/stamped-concrete-patios\">stamped patios</a> page for the pattern options.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
